     writes both, and tools.handbook-release holds the version to VERSION. -->
<date>2026-10-29</date>
<releaseinfo>&reverblabr; 0.1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>&reverblabr; takes sound from your applications, microphones and other
devices, runs it through effects and meters, and sends it where you want it:
to your speakers, to a file, or to another program. It runs on &plasma;, with
PipeWire.</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>Reverblabr</keyword>
<keyword>audio</keyword>
<keyword>PipeWire</keyword>
<keyword>recording</keyword>
<keyword>effects</keyword>
<keyword>virtual device</keyword>
<keyword>hearing</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>In &reverblabr; you build <emphasis>sessions</emphasis> out of
<emphasis>blocks</emphasis>. A session takes sound from sources, runs it
through effects and meters, and sends it to outputs. You drag blocks onto a
canvas, wire them together, and change anything while the sound is
playing.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Sources:</emphasis> a single
application, a microphone or another input device, or everything the computer
plays.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Effects:</emphasis> volume,
compressors and limiters, equalisers, filters, reverb, echo, pitch shift,
noise and hum removal, and more. LV2, CLAP, VST3® and LADSPA plugins
work too.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Meters:</emphasis> peak, RMS and VU
meters at any point in a session.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Outputs:</emphasis> any sound card, a
file, or a virtual device that another program records
from.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Several sessions can run at the same time, in a background engine, with
the window closed. The tray icon shows what is running, global shortcuts
control sessions from anywhere, a session can start by itself when you log
in, and &ctl; runs and changes sessions from a terminal or a script.</para>

<para>&reverblabr; can also make <link linkend="virtual-devices">virtual
devices</link>: sound cards with no hardware behind them, so that you can put
a session between your programs and your real speakers or microphone. And the
blocks of the <link linkend="hearing">Hearing</link> group shape the sound for
each ear on its own, for people who hear part of the sound poorly.</para>

<sect1 id="first-session">
<title>A first session</title>

<para>This walks you through a first session: the sound of one application,
measured, turned up or down, compressed, measured again and sent to your
speakers. It takes about five minutes.</para>

<para>Start &reverblabr; from a &plasma; Wayland session. It opens with one
empty session called <guilabel>Untitled</guilabel>. Start something playing
too: a video in your browser, or music in any player.</para>

<procedure>
<title>Building the session</title>
<step><para>Add these blocks one after another, by clicking each in the
library on the left. A block you click is added after the selected block, and
with automatic connections on (they are, to begin with) it is wired up as
well:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Application</guilabel>, under
<guilabel>Sources</guilabel></para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Peak/RMS</guilabel>, under
<guilabel>Meters</guilabel></para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Volume</guilabel>, under <guilabel>Built-in
Effects</guilabel></para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Dynamics Processor</guilabel>, under
<guilabel>Built-in Effects</guilabel></para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Peak/RMS</guilabel> again</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Output Device</guilabel>, under
<guilabel>Outputs</guilabel></para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<para>That is one lane of six blocks, wired from left to right. The canvas
scrolls to keep each new block in view, and
<menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Fit to
Window</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>9</keycap></keycombo>) shows the
whole session at once.</para></step>

<step><para>The Application block does not know which program to listen to
yet: its tile says <guilabel>Choose an app</guilabel>. Double-click the tile,
or right-click it and choose <guimenuitem>Settings</guimenuitem>, and its
settings open next to it.</para></step>

<step><para>Click <guibutton>Choose an app</guibutton> and pick your browser
or player. The list has the programs that are playing now and the ones that
played recently, and you can type the name of one that is not running
yet.</para></step>

<step><para>Under <guilabel>Audio device selection</guilabel>, leave
<guilabel>Capture audio played to the default output device</guilabel>.
That is what most people mean by <quote>the sound of an
application</quote>. Press &Esc;, or click an empty part of the canvas, to
close the settings.</para></step>

<step><para>The Output Device block starts on <guilabel>Default
output</guilabel>, which is whatever your desktop plays to, and follows along
if you change that. Press <guibutton>Run</guibutton>, or &Space;.</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>The button turns orange and says <guibutton>Stop</guibutton>, and the
clock starts. The two meters show the level before and after the processing,
the Dynamics tile draws its curve with a dot where the sound is on it, and
the wires turn orange while sound goes through them.</para>

<para>You now hear the application twice. &reverblabr; takes a copy of an
application's sound and leaves the original alone, so the program goes on
playing as it always did. To hear only the processed sound:</para>

<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>In the Application block's settings, under <guilabel>Audio
device selection</guilabel>, choose <guilabel>Capture audio played to all
output devices</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>In the &plasma; audio applet, move the application to an
output you are not listening to, such as an HDMI output with nothing plugged
in.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Leave the session's Output Device on your
speakers.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>

<para>Do not turn down the application's own volume instead: PipeWire applies
that volume before &reverblabr; gets the sound, so the copy would go quiet too.
A cleaner way, especially for several programs at once, is a <link
linkend="virtual-devices">virtual device</link>.</para>

<para>Everything can be changed while the session runs, without clicks or
gaps. Drag the slider on the Volume tile or in its settings. It stops at
100 %, the level the sound arrived at; to make the sound louder, choose an
<guilabel>Overdrive</guilabel> in its settings, which lets the slider go up to
200, 300 or 400 %. In the
Dynamics Processor's settings, drag the handles on the curve: the upper one is
where compression starts, the lower one where the expander starts.</para>

<para>Press <guibutton>Stop</guibutton> when you are done. Sessions are saved
as you work, so this one will be there the next time you start &reverblabr;.
It comes back stopped: a session never starts making sound just because
&reverblabr; was opened.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="window">
<title>The window</title>

<para>The main window has four parts.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>The top row</term>
<listitem><para>Your sessions, one tab each, with a <guibutton>+</guibutton>
for a new one. On the right it shows what the session in front is doing, how
long it has been running, and the <guibutton>Run</guibutton> button. This row
is also the window's title bar: drag its empty part to move the window,
double-click it to maximise. At the right end are the dark and light switch,
the <guibutton>☰</guibutton> button with all the menus, the
<guibutton>Virtual Devices</guibutton> button and the usual window buttons.
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo> shows the menus
as a menu bar instead.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The sidebar</term>
<listitem><para>On the left, with four tabs: <guilabel>Blocks</guilabel>,
the library of blocks you can add, grouped by what they do
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>1</keycap></keycombo>);
<guilabel>Files</guilabel>, what the session has recorded (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>2</keycap></keycombo>, see <xref
linkend="files-tab"/>); <guilabel>Info</guilabel>, the session's title,
notes and settings, and the audio system (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo>, see <xref
linkend="info-tab"/>); and <guilabel>Schedule</guilabel>, the timers that
start the session at set times (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>4</keycap></keycombo>, see <xref
linkend="scheduling"/>).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The canvas</term>
<listitem><para>In the middle, where a session's blocks and the wires between
them live.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The status bar</term>
<listitem><para>At the bottom: the rate and buffer size the audio system runs
at, how busy the session is, and how many times it missed its deadline (an
<quote>xrun</quote>, heard as a dropout).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>To have &reverblabr; look like the rest of your desktop, choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Appearance</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Follow
System</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. The window then takes your colours, fonts
and title bar from &plasma;, starting the next time &reverblabr; opens.
<guimenuitem>Reverblabr Dark</guimenuitem> and <guimenuitem>Reverblabr
Light</guimenuitem> are its own looks. In them, text and every mark that means
something (a state dot, a wire, a meter, the keyboard focus) meet the contrast
levels of WCAG 2.1 AA; with Follow System you get your colour scheme's own
colours as they are.</para>

<sect1 id="canvas">
<title>The canvas</title>

<sect2 id="adding-blocks">
<title>Adding and moving blocks</title>

<para>Clicking a block in the library adds it after the selected block. A
block dragged from the library onto a spot on the canvas lands where you
dropped it and is not wired to anything: you picked the place, so &reverblabr;
does not guess. <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></keycombo>
(<menuchoice><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Find
Block…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>) jumps to the library's search: type part of
a name and press &Enter; for the best match. While you type, the list shows the
best matches first: a name that starts with what you typed, then a name with it
in it, then a description.</para>

<para>Drag a block by its title to move it. The rest of the tile is for its
controls, like the Volume slider. To take a block out, select it and press
&Del;.</para>

<para>The canvas zooms with
<menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Zoom In</guimenuitem></menuchoice>
and <guimenuitem>Zoom Out</guimenuitem>; <guimenuitem>Actual Size</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>0</keycap></keycombo>) goes back to
100 %, and <guimenuitem>Fit to Window</guimenuitem> (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>9</keycap></keycombo>) shows the whole
session.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="wiring">
<title>Wiring blocks together</title>

<para>The switch at the top left of the canvas says how a session is wired;
<menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Automatic
Connections</guimenuitem></menuchoice> is the same switch.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>With <emphasis>automatic connections</emphasis>, a block you
add by clicking is wired after the selected one, and taking a block out of the
middle of a lane joins the lane up again behind it. It is the quick way to
build a lane.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>With <emphasis>manual connections</emphasis>, nothing is wired
for you.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Either way, you can wire blocks by hand: hold &Ctrl; and drag from a
block's output connector on its right edge to another block's input connector
on its left edge. The connectors that would accept the wire light up while you
drag. Several wires can go into one input, and they are mixed there; a ring
around the connector shows that. Several sources can feed one lane this way.
To remove a wire, click it and press &Del;.</para>

<para>&reverblabr; refuses a connection that would feed a session's sound back
into its own input, and tells you so, because that sound would go round and
round, louder every time.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="colours">
<title>What the colours mean</title>

<para>The colours follow your &plasma; colour scheme, so they work in light
and dark themes. Orange is the exception: it is &reverblabr;'s own colour and
always means that sound is going through.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Wires</emphasis> are orange and moving
while sound goes through them, and grey when nothing does (the session is
stopped, or the lane is silent).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">The dot</emphasis> in a tile's top
right corner is orange when the block is on and sound goes through it, green
when it is on but nothing goes through at the moment, and grey when the block
is off.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Connectors</emphasis>, the rings on the
edges of a tile, are orange when their block has sound going through
it.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Meters</emphasis> show the level in
decibels and say <guilabel>Idle</guilabel> when no sound reaches them. A meter
that clips lights up and stays lit until you click it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>The switch at the top of a block's settings turns the block on and off.
A block that is off is drawn faded. In the middle of a lane the sound simply
goes past it, as if it were not there. At the start or the end of a lane there
is nothing to go past, so it is silent instead: a source that is off produces
nothing, and an Output Device that is off sends silence to its device. A
source keeps listening while it is off, so switching it back on is
instant.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="settings-and-presets">
<title>A block's settings, presets and undo</title>

<para>Double-click a tile, press &Enter; on it, or right-click it and choose
<guimenuitem>Settings</guimenuitem>, and its settings open next to it.
&Esc; closes them. The window button at the top of the settings pulls them off
the block into a window of their own, which stays where you put it while you
work on other blocks; the pin button puts it back.</para>

<para>The row at the bottom of a block's settings holds its presets. Some
blocks come with presets, and <menuchoice><guimenu>⋮</guimenu><guimenuitem>Save
as Preset…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> keeps your own.
<menuchoice><guimenu>⋮</guimenu><guimenuitem>Reset to Default
Settings…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> puts every setting of the block back to
how a new block starts, after asking; one undo takes it back. A password or a
stream key stays, and so does which ear a hearing block is for. A plugin
block has no reset: its own presets are the way back to where it
started.</para>

<para>The question-mark button beside <guimenu>⋮</guimenu> opens the block's
help in a window of its own, which stays above the others while you work: what
the block is for, how it works, what each of its settings does, when to use it,
and a few tips. One help window serves every block; pressing the button on
another block turns its page.</para>

<para><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Z</keycap></keycombo> takes back
any change, including moving a block or a wire, and the
<guimenu>Edit</guimenu> menu tells you which change it would undo.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="keyboard">
<title>Without a mouse</title>

<para>Everything in a session can be done from the keyboard, and the canvas
works with a screen reader.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>Add a block</term>
<listitem><para><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></keycombo>
jumps to the library's search. Type part of a name and press &Enter; for the
best match, which the list shows first, or walk the list with the arrow
keys.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Move between blocks</term>
<listitem><para>&Tab; and <keycombo action="simul">&Shift;&Tab;</keycombo> go
from block to block. A screen reader says each block's name, whether it is on
and what it is wired to.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Wire two blocks</term>
<listitem><para>On a block, press <keycap>C</keycap>. The wire aims at the
first input that would take it, the arrow keys move it to the next one,
&Enter; connects and &Esc; cancels.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Remove a wire</term>
<listitem><para>On a block, press <keycap>D</keycap>, pick the wire with the
arrow keys and press &Enter; or &Del;.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Settings</term>
<listitem><para>&Enter; on a block opens its settings, &Esc; closes
them.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Run and stop</term>
<listitem><para>&Space;.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The session as a list</term>
<listitem><para><menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show as
List</guimenuitem></menuchoice> replaces the canvas with a list of the blocks,
lane by lane, which is often easier to follow with a screen reader. The same
keys work there, and the choice is remembered.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Meters are read out as progress bars named after their block, and a
clipping meter is announced once, not on every hit.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="info-tab">
<title>The Info tab</title>

<para>The <guilabel>Info</guilabel> tab of the sidebar (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo>) holds what a session is
and how it runs:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Title</guilabel> and <guilabel>Notes</guilabel>, for
what the session is for.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Automatic connections</guilabel> and <guilabel>Show
as list</guilabel>, the same switches as in the menus.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Sample rate</guilabel>, which is always the audio
system's, with the rate in use now beside it, and
<guilabel>Latency</guilabel>, how little delay the session asks the audio
system for: <guilabel>Default</guilabel>, <guilabel>Low</guilabel> or
<guilabel>Very low</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Auto Run</guilabel>, which starts the session when
you log in, with a line under it saying when it starts again after you stop
it; see <xref linkend="auto-run"/>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Audio system</guilabel>: the audio server, its
session manager, the rate and cycle the graph runs at, and whether
&reverblabr; can reach it, with <guilabel>Diagnostics</guilabel> underneath;
see <xref linkend="diagnostics"/>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="blocks">
<title>Blocks</title>

<para>A session is made of blocks: sources that bring sound in, effects and
meters in the middle, and outputs at the end. This chapter lists them in the
order the library groups them. Every block has settings of its own, which open
next to it on the canvas, and every block can save presets.</para>

<sect1 id="blocks-sources">
<title>Sources</title>

<sect2 id="block-source-application">
<title>Application</title>
<para>The sound of one program while it plays. Under <guilabel>Audio device
selection</guilabel> it takes everything the program plays, only what it
plays to the default output, or only what it plays to devices you choose. The
program keeps playing to its own device as before, so you hear it twice unless
you move it elsewhere (see <xref linkend="first-session"/>). The program can
be one that is not running yet: the block picks it up when it starts.</para>
<para>An Application block can also be set to open a web address when its
session starts. That address is opened by your browser or player, not by
&reverblabr;.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-source-device">
<title>Input Device</title>
<para>A microphone or any other recording device, or a <link
linkend="virtual-devices">virtual output</link> that other programs play into.
<guilabel>Default input</guilabel> follows whatever your desktop records from;
choose a device by name to keep it fixed. Virtual devices are listed at the
end, under <guilabel>Virtual devices</guilabel>.</para>
<para>A wireless headset's microphone works only in the headset's headset
profile (HSP/HFP). The system normally switches to it as soon as a session
records from the headset; if the headset stays on its playback profile (A2DP)
and the session hears nothing, the block's settings say so, and the profile
can be changed in the system's sound settings.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-source-system">
<title>System Audio</title>
<para>Everything the computer plays, in one block. You can leave programs out
in its settings. Alert sounds are left out unless you ask for them.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-outputs">
<title>Outputs</title>

<sect2 id="block-output-wave">
<title>Wave</title>
<para>A test tone (sine, square, triangle or sawtooth, 20 Hz to 20 kHz) or
white or pink noise, at the level you set, on the left, the right or both.
Wire it to an Output Device to check a speaker or a cable.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-output-device">
<title>Output Device</title>
<para>Where a lane is heard: the default output (followed when it changes), a
device you choose, or a virtual microphone that other programs record from.
It has a volume of its own. When the session records from a virtual device,
choose your real speakers by name here: left on <guilabel>Default
output</guilabel>, it could follow the default to the very device the session
records from, and &reverblabr; refuses that connection.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-output-broadcast">
<title>Broadcast</title>
<para>Sends what reaches it to an internet radio server, Icecast or SHOUTcast,
as MP3, Opus, Ogg Vorbis or AAC, and passes the sound on unchanged. It
connects when the session runs and lets go when it stops. Its settings have
three tabs:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Setup</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The kind of server, its host and port, and for Icecast the
mount point and the user (usually <literal>source</literal>). A SHOUTcast
server takes its stream on the port after the one you give, as SHOUTcast
always has. The password is kept in your desktop's password store
(&kwallet; on &plasma;), never in the session file: a session you copy or
send to somebody carries none. Then the format, the bit rate, and stereo or
mono.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Metadata</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The station's name, genre, web page and description, and
whether the server may list it in a public directory. The title listeners
see is made from a template: <literal>%artist</literal>,
<literal>%title</literal> and <literal>%album</literal> are what is playing,
and <literal>%session</literal> is the session's name. Text between two of
them is left out when either is empty, so <literal>%artist - %title</literal>
with no artist is the title alone. What is playing can come from a text file
whose first line is the title, which many players can write; it is read again
whenever it changes. Or it can come from a media player, such as Elisa or
VLC: choose <guilabel>A media player</guilabel>, then the player. <guilabel>The
one this session captures</guilabel> follows a player the session's
Application blocks capture, so that the title is that of the music listeners
hear; a session that captures no application, such as one taking a device or
everything the computer plays, follows the player that is playing. Of several
that fit, one that is playing comes before one that is paused, and the one
that started playing last before the others. A player you name is followed
whatever the session captures, and is kept as the choice while it is not
running. A new title reaches the stream within a couple of seconds; a stopped
player leaves only what the template says without it.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Status</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How long it has been connected, how much it has sent, the bit
rate it is sending at, how many times it has connected again, and what last
went wrong.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>When the server goes away, or the network does, the block tries again
after a second, then after twice as long each time, up to once a minute. A
wrong password, a missing password or a format this system cannot make stops
it until you change the setting, and says so. Icecast 2.4 shows no title for
an Opus stream on its own pages; listeners' players show it.</para>
<para>The block is there in a build made with QtKeychain, which keeps its
password.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-output-livestream">
<title>Live Stream</title>
<para>Sends what reaches it to a video streaming service over RTMP or RTMPS,
with a picture made for it, and passes the sound on unchanged. Services that
take a live stream want video, so the block draws one: words, pictures, and
something that moves with the sound. It connects when the session runs and
lets go when it stops. Its settings have three tabs:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Setup</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The service's address, as it gives it
(<literal>rtmps://</literal> where it offers it, which is encrypted and checks
that the server is the one named, or <literal>rtmp://</literal>), and the
stream key, which is kept in your desktop's password store (&kwallet; on
&plasma;), never in the session file. The quality: 1024 × 576 at 15 frames a
second for a slow connection, 1280 × 720 at 30, or 1920 × 1080 at 30. The
encoder: the best one this computer has, unless you choose one. A graphics
card's encoder costs the processor nothing; x264 and OpenH264 encode on the
processor. Only the encoders that work here are offered.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Picture</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The title (the session's name until you change it), a
subtitle, and what is playing, made from a template as the Broadcast block's
title is and filled from a text file or a media player in the same way. A
waveform, a spectrum or a level meter moves with the sound, or nothing does.
The background is a colour, or a picture that covers the frame, and a logo
can sit at its top left. The words are light or dark, whichever reads best
over what is behind them.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term><guilabel>Status</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How long it has been on the air, how much it has sent and at
what rate, the picture's size, frame rate and encoder, how many frames it has
sent and how many it left out because the computer fell behind, how many times
it has connected again, and what last went wrong.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>The picture keeps time with the sound however long the stream runs. When
the service goes away the block tries again, as the Broadcast block does. A
missing key or a computer with no H.264 encoder that works stops it until you
change something, and says so. On Fedora, H.264 comes from Cisco's OpenH264
(the <literal>openh264</literal> package, which Fedora Workstation installs
from Cisco's repository), from the full FFmpeg in RPM Fusion, or from a
graphics card whose driver can encode it.</para>
<para>The block is there in a build made with QtKeychain, FreeType, HarfBuzz
and fontconfig.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-output-recorder">
<title>Recorder</title>
<para>Writes what reaches it to a file and passes the sound on unchanged. See
<xref linkend="recording"/>.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-effects">
<title>Built-in Effects</title>

<sect2 id="block-effect-equalizer10">
<title>10-Band EQ</title>
<para>Ten sliders from 31 Hz to 16 kHz, ±12 dB each, with twenty presets, a
preamp and an option to keep the overall level where it was.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-autolevel">
<title>Auto Level</title>
<para>Brings quiet passages up towards a steady loudness and leaves loud ones
alone, with a limiter so that the lift never clips. For a source whose
loudness keeps changing.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-balance">
<title>Balance</title>
<para>Turns the left or the right side of a stereo lane down against the
other, all the way to silence.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-basstreble">
<title>Bass &amp; Treble</title>
<para>Lifts or cuts the low and the high end by up to 12 dB, to warm a source
up or brighten it.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-channels">
<title>Channels</title>
<para>Swaps the sides, folds to mono, copies one side to both, mutes or
inverts a side, or keeps only what differs between them, fading from one
setting to the next.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-dynamics">
<title>Dynamics Processor</title>
<para>A compressor for loud sound and an expander for quiet sound, drawn as a
curve you edit directly. The upper handle is where compression starts, the
lower one where the expander starts, and a dot shows where the sound is on the
curve while you draw it.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-echo">
<title>Echo</title>
<para>Repeats the sound from a millisecond to two seconds later, each repeat
quieter and duller than the one before, mixed in behind the original.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-highpass">
<title>High Pass Filter</title>
<para>Rolls off rumble, handling noise and the thump of a room below a cutoff
between 20 Hz and 2 kHz, at 12 or 24 dB per octave.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-limiter">
<title>Limiter</title>
<para>Keeps a lane under a ceiling between −12 and 0 dB, including peaks that
fall between samples, and shows how hard it is working.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-lowpass">
<title>Low Pass Filter</title>
<para>Rolls off hiss and other high noise above a cutoff, at 12 or 24 dB per
octave.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-monoenhancer">
<title>Mono Enhancer</title>
<para>Gives a mono recording some width above a frequency you choose, keeps
the bass in the middle, and still sounds right when played back in
mono.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-multibandcompressor">
<title>Multiband Compressor</title>
<para>Three compressors side by side, for the lows, the middle and the highs.
A loud bass does not pull the voice down with it, and a harsh
<quote>s</quote> can be tamed on its own. Drag the lines between the bands to
move where they meet. With every ratio at 1:1, the sound comes out as it went
in.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-pan">
<title>Pan</title>
<para>Places a lane anywhere between left and right. The sound is folded to
mono first and keeps the same loudness wherever you put it.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-pitchshift">
<title>Pitch Shift</title>
<para>Moves the pitch up or down, by up to an octave in whole steps and a
semitone more with cents, without making the sound faster or slower. <guilabel>Semitones</guilabel> moves it in whole steps, like
the keys of a piano. <guilabel>Cents</guilabel> are hundredths of a semitone on
top, for tuning one sound to another. The sound comes out about 60 ms late,
and a new setting fades in over a few tens of milliseconds, so a change never
clicks.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-reverb">
<title>Reverb</title>
<para>Puts the sound in a room. Start from one of the presets in its settings
(<guilabel>Small room</guilabel>, <guilabel>Hall</guilabel> or
<guilabel>Plate</guilabel>), then adjust:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Decay</guilabel> is how long the room rings after
the sound stops, from a tenth of a second to twenty seconds.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Size</guilabel> is how big the room is. Small rooms
ring densely and close, large ones spread out.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Damping</guilabel> makes the top end of the ring
fade sooner, the way soft walls and air do.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Pre-delay</guilabel> holds the room back for up to a
quarter of a second, so you hear the sound clearly before the room joins
in.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Width</guilabel> spreads the room from the middle
out to both sides, and <guilabel>Mix</guilabel> sets how loud it is against
the original.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>The original goes straight through, so a Reverb does not delay the
lane.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-simplecompressor">
<title>Simple Compressor</title>
<para>Steadies the level with one choice (<guilabel>Gentle</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Moderate</guilabel>, <guilabel>Heavy</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Voice</guilabel> or <guilabel>Broadcast</guilabel>) instead of six
controls, and shows what that choice sets.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-effect-volume">
<title>Volume</title>
<para>Turns a lane up or down, to match it to the others. Drag the slider on
its tile or in its settings. It stops at 100 %, the level the sound arrived
at, unless you choose an <guilabel>Overdrive</guilabel> (2×, 3× or 4×) in its
settings, which lets it go up to 200, 300 or 400 %. A limiter keeps the louder
sound from clipping.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-meters">
<title>Meters</title>

<para>Either meter can also be shown in the system tray: tick <guilabel>Show in
system tray</guilabel> in its settings (see <xref linkend="tray"/>).</para>

<sect2 id="block-meter-peakrms">
<title>Peak/RMS</title>
<para>A level meter for any point in a lane, with peak hold and clip
lights.</para>
<para>Each side's bar shows the average level (RMS), with a paler bar above
it that jumps up to each peak. If that bar is more of a distraction than a
help, untick <guilabel>Show peak bar</guilabel> in the meter's settings: the
tile, the settings and the system tray then show the RMS bar alone. The line
that holds the last peak stays, and <guilabel>Hold peaks</guilabel> still
sets how long it waits.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-meter-vu">
<title>VU</title>
<para>A pair of needles that follow the average level, the way a tape
machine's meters did, against a reference level you choose.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-advanced">
<title>Advanced</title>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-declick">
<title>Declick</title>
<para>Takes out clicks, pops and crackle (from a record, a bad cable or a
digital dropout) and fills in what was underneath from the sound on either
side.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-dehum">
<title>Dehum</title>
<para>Removes the 50 or 60 Hz hum of the mains and the whine of its harmonics,
and leaves everything in between alone. With <guilabel>Auto</guilabel> on, it
follows a hum whose frequency drifts.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-denoise">
<title>Denoise</title>
<para>Listens to a few seconds of a steady noise on its own (a fan, hiss, an
air conditioner), then removes that noise from everything that follows. The
noise it learned is saved with the session.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-ducking">
<title>Ducking</title>
<para>Turns a background source down while the main one (usually a voice) is
heard, and back up when it stops. Its tile has two inputs: main and
background.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-firfilter">
<title>FIR Filter</title>
<para>Applies an impulse response from a file: the sound of a room, a speaker
cabinet, or a correction for a pair of headphones. Mono, stereo or true
stereo, resampled to the session's rate, with about 5 ms of delay.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-inputswitch">
<title>Input Switch</title>
<para>Passes one of two inputs, A or B, and crossfades to the other at a
click: half a second, or two seconds with &Shift;. A <link
linkend="global-shortcuts">global shortcut</link> or &ctl; can switch it
too.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-mixer">
<title>Mixer</title>
<para>Five inputs on five strips with volume up to 200 %, mute, solo, balance
and timed fades, under one master, plus five scenes to save and recall a set
of levels.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-parametriceq">
<title>Parametric EQ</title>
<para>Up to ten bands you place yourself (bells, shelves, pass filters,
notches), dragged along the curve they draw, with the live spectrum behind
it.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-speechdenoise">
<title>Speech Denoise</title>
<para>Clears the background from a voice without learning anything first,
using RNNoise, a model trained on speech. It works at 48 kHz and adds 30 ms of
delay. It is only there where the distribution packages RNNoise, which Fedora
does.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-sync">
<title>Sync</title>
<para>Holds a lane back by an exact number of milliseconds, up to two seconds,
to line its sound up with a picture or with another lane.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-advanced-timeshift">
<title>Time Shift</title>
<para>Pauses and rewinds sound that is still arriving, such as a radio stream
or a call. Jump back 3, 10 or 30 seconds, forward again, or straight back to
live. It keeps up to an hour: up to ten minutes in memory, a longer
history on disk.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-hearing">
<title>Hearing</title>

<para>These blocks work on one ear at a time. <xref linkend="hearing"/>
describes them in full.</para>

<sect2 id="block-hearing-compensation16">
<title>Hearing Compensation 16</title>
<para>Lifts soft sound for one ear in sixteen bands, a lot for soft sound and
little for loud sound, under a ceiling nothing goes above. It can be set band
by band or from an audiogram. See <xref
linkend="hearing-compensation-16"/>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-hearing-compensation64">
<title>Hearing Compensation 64</title>
<para>The same in sixty-four bands, set with points on smooth curves, for
steep slopes and narrow dips. See <xref
linkend="hearing-compensation-64"/>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-hearing-frequencylowering">
<title>Frequency Lowering</title>
<para>Moves sound from a high range the ear does not hear into a lower one it
does, so that the hiss of s, sh and f comes back, with about 21 ms of delay.
See <xref linkend="frequency-lowering"/>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-hearing-frequencyraising">
<title>Frequency Raising</title>
<para>The other way round: moves sound from a low or middle range up into one
the ear hears, with about 43 ms of delay, or 85 ms while a move starts below
250 Hz. Experimental in parts. See <xref linkend="frequency-raising"/>.</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="block-hearing-joinlr">
<title>Join L/R</title>
<para>Puts two lanes back together as one stereo pair, the left side of one
and the right side of the other, lined up in time, so that each ear can have
its own blocks. See <xref linkend="one-ear"/>.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="blocks-plugins">
<title>Plugins</title>

<para>&reverblabr; can run plugins that are already installed on your
computer: <emphasis role="strong">LV2</emphasis>, <emphasis
role="strong">CLAP</emphasis> (where the build has the CLAP SDK), <emphasis
role="strong">VST3</emphasis> (where the build has the VST3 SDK 3.8 or later)
and <emphasis role="strong">LADSPA</emphasis>. The library lists what it found
under <guilabel>Plugins</guilabel>, a heading for each format with the plugins
under it in alphabetical order; click a heading to fold it away. The search
looks through all of them, by name first and then by maker and kind, and shows
the best matches first. <guibutton>Look for plugins again</guibutton> looks
again after you install something new. LSP's plugins are a good first set to
try.</para>

<para>Every plugin runs in a separate process, so a plugin that crashes or
hangs takes nothing else down with it. Its block is bypassed, the sound goes
on without it, and the block shows a button to restart it. A plugin's settings
appear in a panel built from the parameters it offers.</para>

<para>An LV2, CLAP or VST3 plugin that has a window of its own shows it with
<guibutton>Plugin Window</guibutton>, and the panel and the window follow each
other. A knob turned in the window is one step for
<guimenuitem>Undo</guimenuitem>, however long it was held. The plugin's own
presets are listed under <guilabel>Plugin presets</guilabel>: an LV2 plugin's
are the ones other LV2 programs see, and a VST3 plugin's are its
<filename>.vstpreset</filename> files in <filename>~/.vst3/presets</filename>
and the system's preset folders. <guibutton>Save…</guibutton> keeps the
settings as one of them, where other programs that use the plugin find it
too.</para>

<para>VST is a registered trademark of Steinberg Media Technologies
GmbH.</para>

<para><guilabel>Test Plugin</guilabel> in the same group is a plain gain built
into &reverblabr;, which runs the way every plugin does. It is there to check
that plugins run on your computer at all.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="recording">
<title>Recording</title>

<sect1 id="recorder">
<title>The Recorder block</title>

<para>Put a <guilabel>Recorder</guilabel> (under
<guilabel>Outputs</guilabel>) anywhere in a lane. It writes what reaches it
to a file and passes the sound on unchanged, so the lane can continue to your
speakers after it. Several Recorders can hang off the same sound, each writing
its own file.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>It records while the session runs</term>
<listitem><para>The tile shows the format, a red <guilabel>REC</guilabel> with
the time, and <guibutton>Pause</guibutton> and <guibutton>Split</guibutton>
buttons. Split ends the current file and starts the next one, numbered on.
Pause leaves the paused time out and joins the two parts.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The file name</term>
<listitem><para>Built from <guilabel>Session</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Date</guilabel>, <guilabel>Time</guilabel> and
<guilabel>Counter</guilabel> pieces plus your own text, and the settings show
what the next file will be called. Files go to a Reverblabr folder in your
Music folder unless you choose another.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Quality</term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Voice</guilabel> (Opus), <guilabel>Standard
MP3</guilabel>, <guilabel>High-quality MP3</guilabel>,
<guilabel>High-quality AAC</guilabel>, <guilabel>Lossless</guilabel> (FLAC)
and <guilabel>Uncompressed</guilabel> (WAV). The format options let you set
the bit rate, bit depth, sample rate and mono by hand. AAC and Apple Lossless
are only offered if your system's FFmpeg can write them; on Fedora the full
FFmpeg comes from RPM Fusion. FLAC and the other formats always
work.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Tags</term>
<listitem><para>Title, artist, album and so on are written into each file when
it is finished. The name pieces work in tags too, so a Track of
<userinput>%counter</userinput> numbers the files of a split
recording.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Limits</term>
<listitem><para>Start a new file every so many minutes, hours or megabytes,
stop the recording after a while, or act on silence: leave it out, start a
new file after it, or stop.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>A crash does not cost you the recording</term>
<listitem><para>The file plays as it is up to the last few seconds, and
&reverblabr; restores the rest the next time it starts.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>A <link linkend="global-shortcuts">global shortcut</link> and
<userinput>reverblabr-ctl split</userinput> start a new file too. Before you
record anyone but yourself, read <xref linkend="privacy"/>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="files-tab">
<title>The Files tab</title>

<para>The <guilabel>Files</guilabel> tab of the sidebar (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>2</keycap></keycombo>) lists what the session has
recorded, newest first, with each file's name, its outline, its length, format
and size. A file still being recorded has a red dot.</para>

<para>To listen to a recording, select it and press its play button, or
&Space;. Click the outline to listen from that point. It plays on your default
output like any music player would, and a running session carries on as it
was. Right-click a recording for more:</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Open Containing Folder</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Opens your file manager with the file
selected.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Open With…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Offers the programs that can open it, such as an audio
editor.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Move to Trash</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Moves it to the trash, where you can restore it from. &Del;
does the same.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>Double-click a recording, or press &Enter;, to open it in the
<guilabel>Recording Inspector</guilabel>, a window of its own. You can rename
the file there, fill in its tags and press <guibutton>Save Tags</guibutton> to
write them into the file. &Space; plays and pauses, and the &Left; and &Right;
arrow keys jump five seconds back or forward.</para>

<para>A file you delete or move in your file manager disappears from the list
the next time you open the tab.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="desktop">
<title>On the desktop</title>

<para>&reverblabr; is meant to sit in the background and get on with it:
sessions keep running with the window closed, the tray and a panel widget
show what is going on, and shortcuts work from anywhere. Most of this needs an installed
&reverblabr;, because a build started from the source tree has no background
engine.</para>

<sect1 id="all-sessions">
<title>All your sessions at once</title>

<para><menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>All
Sessions…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> opens a window listing every session you
have saved, open or not: what each one captures, how many recordings it has
made, how loud it is now, and how long it has been running.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Run</guibutton> or <guibutton>Stop</guibutton> a
session with the button at the end of its row. To start several together,
select them (&Ctrl;-click or &Shift;-click) and press
<guibutton>Run</guibutton> at the top. A session that was not open opens in a
tab of the main window as it starts.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Double-click a session to open it in the main window.
<guibutton>New Session</guibutton> makes one, and
<guibutton>Delete…</guibutton> deletes the selected one after
asking.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click a column header to sort by it, drag a header to move its
column, and choose which columns show with <guibutton>Columns</guibutton>.
The window remembers all of it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>The launcher knows about this too: right-click &reverblabr; in the
application launcher or the task manager for <guimenuitem>New
Session</guimenuitem> and <guimenuitem>All Sessions</guimenuitem>. There is
only ever one &reverblabr; running, so starting it again just brings its
window forward.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="auto-run">
<title>Sessions that run when you log in</title>

<para>Some sessions you want running all the time, such as a microphone chain
for calls or a hearing setup. Switch on <guilabel>Auto Run: Run when I log
in</guilabel> in the session's <link linkend="info-tab">Info</link> tab, or
tick it in <guimenuitem>All Sessions…</guimenuitem>.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>From then on, the background engine starts that session when
you log in, without opening the window, and again if the engine crashes and
has to restart. Closing the window leaves it running.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Stopping the session stops it until the next login, even when
the engine is started again later by the window, by &ctl; or by &krunner;.
Undo takes the setting back like any other change.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>If you would rather have these sessions start again whenever
the background engine starts, after you stopped them too, choose
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Auto Run</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Every
Time the Background Engine Starts</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. The engine stops
by itself a minute after nothing is using it, so a session you stopped then
starts again the next time anything wakes the engine, such as opening the
window or typing <userinput>rb</userinput> in &krunner;. The choice is the
same for every session, and holds from the next time the engine starts.
<guimenuitem>Once per Login: Stopped Sessions Stay Stopped</guimenuitem> is
the default.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>When no session is set to run at login, nothing of
&reverblabr; starts when you log in.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>It needs an installed &reverblabr;. In a build run from the
source tree, the Info tab says Auto Run cannot work there, and the session
keeps the setting for later.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="scheduling">
<title>Sessions on a schedule</title>

<para>The <guilabel>Schedule</guilabel> tab of the sidebar (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>4</keycap></keycombo>) starts a session at the
times you set, such as a radio programme every weekday morning or a call to
record once on a date, and stops it again when the time is up.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Add Timer</guibutton> (the <guibutton>+</guibutton>)
asks how often: <guilabel>Once</guilabel> on a date still to come, <guilabel>Every
day</guilabel>, <guilabel>Monday to Friday</guilabel>, or <guilabel>On these
days</guilabel> for the days you choose. It also asks the time it starts and
how long it runs. <guibutton>Save</guibutton> waits until the timer is one that
can run, and the dialog shows the calendar expression systemd is
given.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>When done: Quit the applications this session
captures</guilabel> also asks each application the session records from to
quit when the run is over, as logging out would. &reverblabr; asks you to
confirm before it sets this, since anything unsaved in those applications may
be lost.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Each timer has a switch to turn it off without deleting it,
and buttons to edit or delete it. Undo takes back any of these, like any other
change to the session. The timers are kept in the session's file, so they go
with it when it is exported, and an imported copy gets timers of its
own.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">&reverblabr; does not need to be
open.</emphasis> The background engine hands each timer that is on to systemd
as a user timer, which starts the engine and the session when the time comes.
<userinput>systemctl --user list-timers 'reverblabr-timer-*'</userinput> lists
them, and <userinput>reverblabr-ctl timers list</userinput> says the same with
the sessions' names (see <xref linkend="ctl"/>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A session that is already running when its timer comes round
is left as it is, and is not stopped at the end either. Stopping a session
yourself ends its timer's run.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The computer has to be on and you logged in: a timer does not
wake it from sleep. One whose time came while the computer was asleep does not
start the session when it wakes, unless that is within two minutes of the
time, and a session that is running when the computer goes to sleep is stopped
when it wakes, if its time is up by then.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>It needs an installed &reverblabr;, as <link
linkend="auto-run">Auto Run</link> does. In a build run from the source tree
the Schedule tab says so, and the session keeps its timers for
later.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tray">
<title>The tray icon</title>

<para>While &reverblabr; runs, its icon sits in the system tray. With nothing
running it waits among the hidden icons, and it moves into view while a
session runs.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Click it</emphasis> to see every saved
session, how loud each one is, and <guibutton>Run</guibutton> or
<guibutton>Stop</guibutton> for each. Meters you marked <guilabel>Show in
system tray</guilabel> in a Peak/RMS or VU meter's settings are listed there
as well.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Middle-click it</emphasis> to show or
hide the main window. Its menu has <guimenuitem>Show Reverblabr</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>All Sessions…</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Quit</guimenuitem>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">A red dot</emphasis> on the icon means
something is being recorded. <emphasis role="strong">A warning
sign</emphasis> means something went wrong; click the icon to read
what.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Closing the main window does not quit &reverblabr; while there is a
tray. Your sessions keep running and the icon stays. To quit, use
<guimenuitem>Quit</guimenuitem> in the icon's menu. Sessions running in the
background engine keep going even then; only sessions running inside the
window stop.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="panel-widget">
<title>The panel widget</title>

<para><guilabel>Reverblabr Sessions</guilabel> puts your sessions in a &plasma;
panel, whether the window is open or not. Right-click the panel, choose
<guimenuitem>Add or Manage Widgets…</guimenuitem>, and drag
<guilabel>Reverblabr Sessions</guilabel> onto the panel (or onto the
desktop).</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">In the panel</emphasis> it is
&reverblabr;'s icon. A green dot means a session is running, a red dot that
something is being recorded. Hover over it to read how many sessions
run.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Click it</emphasis> to see every saved
session, with <guibutton>Run</guibutton> or <guibutton>Stop</guibutton> and how
loud each one is. A session that was not open is opened in the background
engine as it starts; the window does not have to be running.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The dots and the bars take their colours from your &plasma;
theme, light or dark.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>In the system tray, the widget waits among the hidden icons
while nothing runs, like the window's tray icon.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>The widget costs nothing while nothing runs: it reads the levels only
while its list is open and a session is running. Being in the panel does not
start the background engine. Opening its list does, if the engine is not
running yet, to ask it for the sessions, and the engine leaves again a minute
later if you do not start anything.</para>

<para>If the widget starts while a session is already recording, when you add
it or when &plasma; starts, it shows the red dot from that session's next
recording on. The widget needs an installed &reverblabr;; with no background
engine installed, its list says so.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="global-shortcuts">
<title>Global shortcuts</title>

<para>&reverblabr;'s global shortcuts work while the window is in the
background or hidden in the tray. They start out with no keys assigned, so
nothing clashes with your desktop's own shortcuts. Set the keys you want in
&systemsettings;, under <menuchoice><guimenu>Keyboard</guimenu><guisubmenu>Shortcuts</guisubmenu><guimenuitem>Reverblabr</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Run or stop "<replaceable>name</replaceable>"</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>One for every saved session. The shortcut follows its
session: renaming the session keeps the key, deleting it removes the key. A
session that is not open is opened and started.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Show Reverblabr</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Brings the main window to the front.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>Blocks</term>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Input Switch: play A</guilabel> and <guilabel>play
B</guilabel>; <guilabel>Time Shift: pause or play</guilabel>, <guilabel>back
10 seconds</guilabel>, <guilabel>forward 10 seconds</guilabel> and
<guilabel>back to live</guilabel>; <guilabel>Recorder: start a new
file</guilabel>. These act on every block of that kind in the session the
main window shows (or showed last, while it is hidden).</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>The shortcuts belong to the window's process, so they work while it
runs, open or hidden in the tray. Quitting from the tray ends them, even if
sessions keep running in the background engine.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="krunner">
<title>&krunner;</title>

<para>Press <keycombo action="simul">&Alt;&Space;</keycombo> (or start typing
on the desktop) and type <userinput>rb</userinput> followed by part of a
session's name:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><userinput>rb podcast</userinput> offers <guilabel>Run
“Podcast”</guilabel>, or <guilabel>Stop “Podcast”</guilabel> while it runs,
with what it is doing now underneath. Press &Enter; to run or stop it. A
session that is not open is opened in the background engine and run, and stays
open there.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The button beside it, <guibutton>Open in
Reverblabr</guibutton>, opens the session in the main window and brings the
window forward, starting &reverblabr; if it is not running.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><userinput>rb</userinput> on its own lists every saved
session.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><userinput>rb rec</userinput> (or any longer start of
<quote>recording</quote>) says which sessions are recording now, or that
nothing is. Pick one to open it in the main window.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>All of this works with the window closed: &krunner; talks to the
background engine, and starts it if it is not running. Looking something up
does not keep the engine running; it goes away again after a minute if
nothing is running. &krunner; lists it as <guilabel>Reverblabr
Sessions</guilabel> in &systemsettings;, under
<menuchoice><guimenu>Search</guimenu><guimenuitem>Plasma
Search</guimenuitem></menuchoice>, where you can turn it off. It needs an
installed &reverblabr;.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="notifications">
<title>Notifications</title>

<para>&reverblabr; tells your desktop about things you would otherwise
miss:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>a recording finished, with <guibutton>Open</guibutton> and
<guibutton>Show in Folder</guibutton></para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a device disconnected while a session was using it (the
session waits for it and carries on when it is back)</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>an application a session captures stopped playing, and started
again</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a plugin stopped (its block is bypassed until you restart
it)</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>the disk under a recording is nearly full</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a connection that would have fed a session's sound back into
itself was refused</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>A device that comes back within a few seconds, or an application that
pauses briefly between two sounds, is not worth a notification, so you will
not hear about it. The same thing about the same block is reported at most
once a minute.</para>

<para>Each kind of notification is listed under <guilabel>Reverblabr</guilabel>
in &systemsettings;, under <guimenuitem>Notifications</guimenuitem>, where you
can turn it off or give it a sound. Do Not Disturb silences them like any
other.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="virtual-devices">
<title>Virtual devices</title>

<para>A virtual device is a sound card with no hardware behind it. Other
programs see it in their device lists like any other sound card, and a
&reverblabr; session can record from it or play into it. That is what lets
you put a session between your programs and your real sound card, in either
direction:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">A virtual output</emphasis> is
something programs play into. Send your browser, your music player or every
program at once to it, and a session picks the sound up, runs it through
whatever blocks you like, and plays the result on your real speakers or
headphones.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">A virtual input</emphasis> is a
microphone that programs record from. A session takes your real microphone,
cleans it up and plays it into the virtual one, and your call or recording
program uses that as its microphone.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<sect1 id="virtual-devices-window">
<title>Making and deleting devices</title>

<para>Open the <guilabel>Virtual Devices</guilabel> window with its button at
the right end of the top row, or with
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Virtual
Devices…</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. It has two lists,
<guilabel>Outputs</guilabel> and <guilabel>Inputs</guilabel>.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Add Device…</guibutton> asks for a name, the kind
(<guilabel>Output</guilabel> or <guilabel>Input</guilabel>),
<guilabel>Stereo</guilabel> or <guilabel>Mono</guilabel>, and who the device
is for (see <xref linkend="all-users"/>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Each row has a switch, a button to rename the device (or
double-click the name, or press <keycap>F2</keycap>) and a button to delete
it.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A device that is switched off stays in the list, but programs
cannot see it. Programs that were using it move to another
device.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>Virtual devices belong to your desktop, not to a session and not to
&reverblabr;. They stay until you delete them, whether &reverblabr; is running
or not, and come back after a restart. Renaming one does not break anything:
programs playing into it carry on, and blocks that use it keep using it.</para>

<para>They are silent on their own. Sound sent to a virtual output goes
nowhere until a running session picks it up and plays it somewhere.
&reverblabr; never starts a session by itself, so when no session is running,
move your programs back to your real sound card, or set a session to <link
linkend="auto-run">run at login</link>.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="process-programs">
<title>Process the sound of your programs</title>

<procedure>
<step><para>In <guilabel>Virtual Devices</guilabel>, add an
<guilabel>Output</guilabel>, for example <quote>Every
app</quote>.</para></step>
<step><para>In the &plasma; audio applet, make it your default output, or move
only the programs you want to process onto it.</para></step>
<step><para>In a session, add an <guilabel>Input Device</guilabel> block, and
in its settings choose your new device. Virtual devices are listed at the
end, under <guilabel>Virtual devices</guilabel>.</para></step>
<step><para>Add the effects and meters you want after it.</para></step>
<step><para>End the lane with an <guilabel>Output Device</guilabel>, and choose
your real speakers or headphones <emphasis>by name</emphasis>. Left on
<guilabel>Default output</guilabel>, it would follow the default to the
virtual device, which is the session's own input.</para></step>
<step><para>Press <guibutton>Run</guibutton>.</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>Compared with capturing a single application, this way nothing is heard
twice, and you can process several programs together or set up a different
session for each.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="process-microphone">
<title>Process your microphone for other programs</title>

<procedure>
<step><para>In <guilabel>Virtual Devices</guilabel>, add an
<guilabel>Input</guilabel>, for example <quote>Processed voice</quote>.
<guilabel>Mono</guilabel> is right for a voice.</para></step>
<step><para>In a session, add an <guilabel>Input Device</guilabel> block and
choose your real microphone <emphasis>by name</emphasis>. <guilabel>Default
input</guilabel> would follow the default to the virtual microphone, if you
made that the default.</para></step>
<step><para>Add the effects you want, for example a Dynamics Processor or a
Speech Denoise, and a Peak/RMS meter to watch the level.</para></step>
<step><para>End the lane with an <guilabel>Output Device</guilabel>, and choose
your virtual microphone under <guilabel>Virtual devices</guilabel>.</para></step>
<step><para>Press <guibutton>Run</guibutton>.</para></step>
<step><para>In your call or recording program, choose the virtual
microphone.</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>A session like this is a good candidate for <link
linkend="auto-run">Auto Run</link>, so that the processed microphone is simply
there after you log in.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="all-users">
<title>Devices for all users</title>

<para><guilabel>Only Me</guilabel> is the usual choice when you add a device,
and it needs nothing more. <guilabel>All Users</guilabel> makes the device for
every account on the computer, and asks for an administrator's password. The
password is remembered for a few minutes, so a few changes in a row only ask
once. Devices for all users have a padlock in the list. Other users get the
device the next time they log in.</para>

<para>This needs a small helper that runs as root. A full install includes it;
until it is installed, choosing All Users only tells you that it is
missing.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="removing">
<title>Removing &reverblabr;</title>

<para>Uninstalling &reverblabr; does not remove its virtual devices: PipeWire
keeps making them at every login. Delete them in the Virtual Devices window
first. If &reverblabr; is already gone, delete the files PipeWire makes them
from, then log out and back in:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>your own devices:
<filename>~/.config/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/90-reverblabr-virtual-devices.conf</filename></para></listitem>
<listitem><para>devices for all users:
<filename>/etc/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/90-reverblabr-virtual-devices-system.conf</filename>
(as root)</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="hearing">
<title>Hearing</title>

<para>The blocks in the <guilabel>Hearing</guilabel> group of the library are
for people who hear part of the sound poorly: someone who cannot hear the high
frequencies any more, who has a dip somewhere in the middle, or whose two ears
hear differently. They work on one ear at a time, so each ear can be set up
for the way it hears, and they can work from an audiogram.</para>

<warning>
<para><emphasis role="strong">&reverblabr; is not a medical device.</emphasis>
It shapes sound for listening and measures nothing about anybody's health. It
does not replace an audiologist or a hearing aid. If your hearing has changed,
have it checked.</para>
<para><emphasis role="strong">Be careful with loud sound.</emphasis> These
blocks can add a lot of gain. Start with the volume low, especially with
headphones, raise it slowly, and leave the <guilabel>Output ceiling</guilabel>
in place.</para>
</warning>

<sect1 id="one-ear">
<title>One ear at a time</title>

<para>Every block in the Hearing group except Join L/R has an
<guilabel>Ear</guilabel> setting, Left or Right, at the top of its settings,
and its tile shows an <guilabel>L</guilabel> (blue) or an
<guilabel>R</guilabel> (red). The block changes that side only and passes the
other side through, delayed by exactly as much as its own side, so the two
ears stay in step. Changing the Ear while the session runs fades across
instead of clicking, and one undo puts it back. Loading a preset or putting
the block back to its default settings never changes it, so a preset made for
one ear can be tried on the other.</para>

<para>A new block starts on the left ear. When the session already has more
blocks of the same kind for the left ear than for the right, a new one starts
on the right instead, so adding the same block twice gives you one for each
ear.</para>

<para>There are two ways to give each ear blocks of its own:</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">In series, on one lane:</emphasis> a
block set to Left, then the same kind of block set to Right. It is simple, but
the delays of every block on the lane add up.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Side by side, on two lanes:</emphasis>
wire the source to two lanes, one for each ear, and bring them back together
with <guilabel>Join L/R</guilabel>. Its <guilabel>Left</guilabel> input takes
the left side of one lane and its <guilabel>Right</guilabel> input the right
side of the other. &reverblabr; lines the two lanes up where they meet, so the
ears stay in step even when one lane has more blocks than the other, and the
session is only as late as the slower lane.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>To set up two lanes with Hearing Compensation 16 (any hearing block works
the same way):</para>

<procedure>
<step><para>Select the source and click <guilabel>Hearing Compensation
16</guilabel> in the library. It is wired after the source and starts on the
left ear.</para></step>
<step><para>Select the source again and click <guilabel>Hearing Compensation
16</guilabel> once more. The new block gets a lane of its own from the same
source and starts on the right ear.</para></step>
<step><para>Select the left ear's block and click <guilabel>Join
L/R</guilabel>. It takes that block on its <guilabel>Left</guilabel>
input.</para></step>
<step><para>Hold &Ctrl; and drag from the right ear's block to
the <guilabel>Right</guilabel> input of Join L/R.</para></step>
<step><para>Select Join L/R and add the output, such as an Output
Device.</para></step>
</procedure>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="hearing-compensation-16">
<title>Hearing Compensation 16</title>

<para>Hearing Compensation 16 gives one ear more of what it hears faintly. In
each of sixteen bands it has three gains, for <guilabel>soft</guilabel>,
<guilabel>average</guilabel> and <guilabel>loud</guilabel> sound, and a
<guilabel>ceiling</guilabel> that nothing in that band goes above. Soft sound
gets the most lift and loud sound the least, so quiet speech comes up without
loud sound becoming too much.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>The Gain view</guilabel> draws the three gains and
the ceiling across the frequencies. Pick <guilabel>Soft</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Average</guilabel>, <guilabel>Loud</guilabel> or
<guilabel>Ceiling</guilabel> above it, then drag a band's handle up or down.
The dark line is the gain each band is giving right now, with the sound
playing drawn behind it.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>The Levels view</guilabel> shows where the sound sits
for this ear: the quietest levels it hears (from the audiogram), the
ceilings, and the sound going in and coming out.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Click a handle to see that band's settings as
numbers.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Speed</guilabel> decides how quickly the gain follows
the sound. <guilabel>Syllabic</guilabel> follows speech syllable by syllable,
<guilabel>Slow</guilabel> keeps the shape of music, and
<guilabel>Adaptive</guilabel> does both and catches a sudden loud sound at
once.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Loudness</guilabel> turns all bands up or down
together. <guilabel>Noise floor</guilabel> keeps the lift from bringing up the
hiss of a recording. <guilabel>Most gain</guilabel> is the most any band ever
gets. <guilabel>Output ceiling</guilabel> is a limiter at the very
end.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Hold for before</guibutton> plays the sound without
the block for as long as you hold the button, so you can
compare.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>With a block for each ear, each panel draws the other ear's curve
faintly, and <guibutton>Copy from the Other Ear</guibutton> takes over its
settings as a starting point.</para>

<sect2 id="audiogram">
<title>From an audiogram</title>

<para>If you have an audiogram (the chart from a hearing test), you do not
have to set the bands by hand. Open <guilabel>Audiogram</guilabel>, enter the
quietest level heard at each frequency, and, if you know them, the levels
where sound becomes too loud. Then press <guibutton>Fit from
Audiogram</guibutton>. All bands are set in one step, and one undo takes it
back. No band gets more than <guilabel>Most gain</guilabel>, nor more than
50 dB however high Most gain is.</para>

<para>A band where more gain is unlikely to help is marked. Often a better
idea there is to move that sound somewhere the ear hears it, with <link
linkend="frequency-lowering">Frequency Lowering</link>.</para>

<para>The fit follows published fitting rules. Treat the result as a starting
point and adjust by ear.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="hearing-compensation-64">
<title>Hearing Compensation 64</title>

<para>Hearing Compensation 64 works the same way with sixty-four bands, for a
steep slope, a narrow dip or a sharp edge that sixteen bands cannot follow.
Instead of one handle per band you set <emphasis>points</emphasis>, and the
bands follow smooth curves through them that never overshoot.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Drag a point sideways to move it, and up or down to change the
curve you picked above the view.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Double-click an empty spot to add a point (up to 24). It is
placed on the curves as they are, so nothing changes until you move it.
<guibutton>Remove Point</guibutton> takes the selected one
out.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guibutton>Fit from Audiogram</guibutton> places a point at
each frequency the audiogram has a level for.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="frequency-lowering">
<title>Frequency Lowering</title>

<para>Some ears hear nothing in a high range however loud it is, and an
important part of speech lives up there: the hiss of <quote>s</quote>,
<quote>sh</quote> and <quote>f</quote>, which is what tells
<quote>sit</quote> from <quote>fit</quote> and <quote>cat</quote> from
<quote>cats</quote>. Frequency Lowering moves that sound down into a range the
ear does hear.</para>

<procedure>
<step><para>Add it for the ear that needs it and choose that ear under
<guilabel>Ear</guilabel>. Add a second one for the other ear if that one needs
it too: it starts on the right.</para></step>
<step><para>Its first move is ready to go: sound from 4 to 8 kHz squeezed into
2.5 to 4 kHz, with the high range emptied. On the
<guilabel>Arrows</guilabel> view, the shaded range is where the sound comes
from and the outlined one is where it goes. Drag the ends of either range to
change them.</para></step>
<step><para>Choose how each move carries the sound.
<guilabel>Squeeze</guilabel> fits the whole range into a smaller one lower
down: use it where the ear hears nothing at all, and
<guilabel>Empty</guilabel> the range it came from. <guilabel>Copy
down</guilabel> adds a copy lower down and keeps the original, either
<guilabel>As it was</guilabel> or <guilabel>Quieter</guilabel>: use it where
the ear still hears some of the high range. Every sound moves by the same
step, from the bottom of one range to the bottom of the other: about two
thirds of an octave with the ranges a new block starts with.</para></step>
<step><para><guilabel>Acts: On s, sh, f</guilabel> moves sound only while the
high range carries most of it, so vowels and music pass unchanged.
<guilabel>Always</guilabel> moves everything in the range.</para></step>
<step><para>Turn on <guilabel>Moved sound only</guilabel> to hear just what
the moves add while you set their <guilabel>Level</guilabel>, then turn it off
again.</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>Up to four moves can work at once, each from its own range. The
<guilabel>Map</guilabel> view shows each move as a line from the frequency
that goes in to the one that comes out.</para>

<para>Put Frequency Lowering <emphasis>before</emphasis> any block that sets
the level of each range, such as Hearing Compensation, so the moved sound gets
the level of the range it lands in. It delays its ear by about 21 ms, and the
other ear waits as long.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="frequency-raising">
<title>Frequency Raising</title>

<para>Some ears hear the low range faintly or not at all, while the higher
range is fine. Frequency Raising is Frequency Lowering the other way round: it
moves sound from a low or middle range up into one the ear hears. The panel,
the moves and the views are the same.</para>

<procedure>
<step><para>Add it for the ear that needs it and choose that ear under
<guilabel>Ear</guilabel>.</para></step>
<step><para>Its first move is ready: sound from 250 to 500 Hz copied two
octaves up into 1 to 2 kHz, with the original kept.</para></step>
<step><para>Choose how each move carries the sound. <guilabel>Copy
up</guilabel> adds a copy higher up and keeps the original.
<guilabel>Squeeze</guilabel> fits the whole range into another one higher up:
use it where the ear hears nothing at all in the low range, and
<guilabel>Empty</guilabel> the range it came from. <guilabel>Add
harmonics</guilabel> is experimental: instead of raising a voice's pitch, it
leaves the voice where it is and adds its harmonics (twice its frequency,
three times, and so on) wherever they fall in the destination. The ear takes
the pitch from the harmonics, so the voice still sounds like itself. Not much
is known about how well this helps, so trust your ears.</para></step>
<step><para><guilabel>Acts: On low sounds</guilabel> moves sound only while
the low range is louder than everything above it, such as a voice or a hum.
<guilabel>Always</guilabel> moves everything in the range.</para></step>
</procedure>

<para>Frequency Raising needs a longer stretch of sound to tell low sounds
apart, so it delays its ear by as much as its lowest move needs: about 43 ms
while every move starts at 250 Hz or above, and about 85 ms once a move starts
lower. A move switched off still counts, so switching it on and off to compare
does not move the ear in time; take it out of the list to stop it counting.
The panel's <guilabel>Delay</guilabel> says which it is. When a move crosses
250 Hz while the session runs, the lane fades out, stays quiet for about as
long as the new delay, and fades back in: a short gap, not a click. Even 43 ms
is enough to notice with video (lips move before you hear the voice), so it is
better kept for listening.</para>

<sect2 id="dip">
<title>A dip in the middle</title>

<para>Some ears hear the lows and the highs but not a range in the middle. You
can take the dip apart with both blocks, set to the same ear, one after the
other. For a dip from 1 to 4 kHz:</para>

<orderedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Frequency Lowering</emphasis> moves the
lower half of the dip down below it: sound from 1 to 2 kHz squeezed into
500 Hz to 1 kHz.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Frequency Raising</emphasis> moves the
upper half up above it: sound from 2 to 4 kHz squeezed into 4 to
8 kHz.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>

<para>With the halves split like this, the order does not matter. If the
ranges overlap, the second block moves what the first one has already moved,
so put first the move you want the other to work on. Both belong before any
block that sets the level of each range.</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="ctl">
<title>&ctl;</title>

<para>&ctl; runs and changes &reverblabr;'s sessions from a terminal, a
script, cron or a desktop tool. It asks the background engine and waits for
the answer, so a script does not have to. The engine does the work, so a
session changed here changes in every open &reverblabr; window at the same
time. The first call starts the engine if it is installed and not
running.</para>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>reverblabr-ctl start "Evening radio"</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>reverblabr-ctl switch "Evening radio" B</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>reverblabr-ctl set "Evening radio" Voice gain 0.5</userinput>
<prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>reverblabr-ctl split "Evening radio"</userinput></screen>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>list</userinput></term>
<listitem><para>The sessions' names, one a line.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>status <optional><replaceable>session</replaceable></optional></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Every session and its state, or one's.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>start <replaceable>session</replaceable></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Runs it, opening it first if it is only on disk. One already
running is left as it is.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>stop <replaceable>session</replaceable></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Stops it. One not running is left as it is.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>split <replaceable>session</replaceable> <optional><replaceable>recorder</replaceable></optional></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Ends the file each Recorder of a running session is writing,
or the named one's, and starts the next with no gap.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>switch <replaceable>session</replaceable> <optional><replaceable>switch</replaceable></optional> A|B</userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Turns an Input Switch to A or B. The switch may be left out
when the session has only one.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>set <replaceable>session</replaceable> <replaceable>block</replaceable> <replaceable>setting</replaceable> <replaceable>value</replaceable></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Changes one setting, as one step the window can undo. A number
is written with a dot (<userinput>0.5</userinput>, whatever the language), a
switch takes <userinput>true</userinput> or <userinput>false</userinput>, and
a choice takes one of its choices.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>timers list <optional><replaceable>session</replaceable></optional></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Every session's timers, or one's, a line each: when it runs,
for how long, when it next starts its session, and its id in
brackets.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><userinput>timer-fire <replaceable>timer</replaceable></userinput></term>
<listitem><para>Does now what the timer does when its time comes: starts its
session and has the engine stop it once the timer's duration is over. A session
already running is left as it is, and the answer says so. The systemd timer
&reverblabr; keeps for each timer runs this with
<option>--scheduled</option>, which refuses a fire that comes long after its
time; running it yourself is a way to try a timer out.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>A session is named by its name or its id, and a block by the name given
it in the window or its id. A name that matches nothing exactly may match
ignoring case, and a name two sessions or two blocks share is refused, with
their ids, rather than guessed. <option>--json</option> prints each answer as
one line of JSON for scripts, and <option>--no-activate</option> asks only an
engine that is already running.</para>

<para><userinput>man reverblabr-ctl</userinput> describes every command and
option, the exit status and the JSON. bash and zsh complete the commands, the
sessions' names and the timers' ids.</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="privacy">
<title>Privacy</title>

<para>Linux does not ask for permission before one program listens to another
program or to your microphone. &reverblabr; can capture any application,
everything the computer plays and any microphone, and nobody is asked. That
puts the responsibility on you.</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Other people's voices.</emphasis> In
many countries it is against the law to record a phone or video call, or any
conversation, without the agreement of everyone in it, and the rules differ
from place to place. Find out what applies where you and the other people
are, and ask before you record.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">What you capture.</emphasis> System
Audio takes everything that plays, including calls, notifications and
whatever a browser tab is doing. Leave out the programs you do not mean to
capture.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><emphasis role="strong">Seeing who is listening.</emphasis> A
running session shows up by name in PipeWire's own tools, such as
<command>wpctl status</command>, qpwgraph and Helvum, along with what it is
connected to. The &plasma; audio applet does not list it, so look there if you
need to know.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>&reverblabr; itself does not use the network on its own account: no
usage statistics, no update checks. The two blocks that do are Broadcast,
which sends a running session to the radio server you name, with the station
details you give it, and Live Stream, which sends it to the video service you
name, with the picture you set up for it; nothing else goes there. A Broadcast
block's password and a Live Stream block's stream key are kept in your
desktop's password store (&kwallet; on &plasma;), not in the session file. A
password travels to a radio server the way the server's protocol sends it,
which is in the clear unless the server is on a network you trust; a stream key
travels in the clear over <literal>rtmp://</literal> and encrypted over
<literal>rtmps://</literal>. When a Broadcast or Live Stream block's title
comes from a media player, &reverblabr; reads what the players
on your session bus say they are playing (artist, title and album), their
names, and which program each one is, while that session runs, and the window
reads their names to list them; it never controls a player, keeps none of it,
and sends nothing but the title the template makes, to that block's server.
An Application block can be set to open a web
address when its session starts, but that address is opened by your browser or
player, not by &reverblabr;. When you copy diagnostics for a bug report, your
home folder, user name and computer name are left out, but please read the
text before you send it.</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>When something goes wrong</title>

<sect1 id="diagnostics">
<title>Diagnostics</title>

<para>The <guilabel>Info</guilabel> tab (<keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo>) has a
<guilabel>Diagnostics</guilabel> section under <guilabel>Audio
system</guilabel>. Unfold it to see how each open session is keeping up: its
load, how often it was late, and whether it got real-time priority. It also
lists what &reverblabr; is connected to, which recording formats this computer
can write, and how many plugins were found.</para>

<para><guibutton>Copy Diagnostics</guibutton> puts all of that on the
clipboard, together with &reverblabr;'s last log lines, ready to paste into a
bug report. Your home folder, user name and computer name are left out, but
read it through before you send it.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="problems">
<title>Common problems</title>

<qandaset>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>I hear the sound twice.</para></question>
<answer><para>An Application block takes a copy of a program's sound and
leaves the original playing. <xref linkend="first-session"/> shows how to hear
only the processed sound, and <xref linkend="process-programs"/> has the
cleaner way.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A banner says the background engine is not
installed.</para></question>
<answer><para>That is expected when you run &reverblabr; from the build tree:
the window runs the engine itself, and sessions stop when it closes. Install
&reverblabr; to get the background engine.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The sound drops out.</para></question>
<answer><para>Look at the xrun counter in the status bar and at <link
linkend="diagnostics">Diagnostics</link>. A session without real-time priority
is much more likely to drop out when the computer is busy, and Diagnostics
says so. Real-time priority comes from rtkit, which Fedora and Kubuntu run by
default. Heavy plugins, many sessions at once and a very small buffer size in
PipeWire make dropouts more likely too.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>&reverblabr; refused a connection.</para></question>
<answer><para>It refuses anything that would feed a session's sound back into
its own input, because that would howl louder and louder. This usually means
an Output Device is set to <guilabel>Default output</guilabel> while the
default is the virtual device the session records from. Choose your real
speakers by name.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A block is missing from the library.</para></question>
<answer><para>Some blocks are only built when their library was found: Speech
Denoise needs RNNoise, the CLAP block needs the CLAP SDK, the VST3 block needs
the VST3 SDK 3.8 or later. Your distribution's
package, or the build's summary, says what was left out.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A plugin stopped.</para></question>
<answer><para>Plugins run in their own process, so a crash only takes the
plugin down. Its block is bypassed and shows a button to restart it. If it
keeps happening, try the plugin in another host, and say in a bug report
whether it works there.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Starting &reverblabr; only brings the old window
back.</para></question>
<answer><para>Only one &reverblabr; runs at a time, and starting it again hands
over to the one that is running. If an installed &reverblabr; is running, a
build from the source tree just raises that one, so quit it first from the
tray icon's menu.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Virtual devices are still there after
uninstalling.</para></question>
<answer><para>They belong to PipeWire. <xref linkend="removing"/> says which
files to delete.</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="logs">
<title>Logs</title>

<para>To see &reverblabr;'s debug messages, start it from a terminal like
this:</para>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>QT_LOGGING_RULES='reverblabr.*.debug=true' reverblabr</userinput></screen>

<para>The background engine logs to the journal:</para>

<screen><prompt>$</prompt> <userinput>journalctl --user -u reverblabrd</userinput></screen>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="reporting-bugs">
<title>Reporting a bug</title>

<para>Please open an issue at <ulink
url="https://github.com/sorrelfox/reverblabr/issues">https://github.com/sorrelfox/reverblabr/issues</ulink>.
Say what you did, what you expected and what happened instead, and paste what
<guibutton>Copy Diagnostics</guibutton> gives you. Steps that make the problem
happen again are the most useful thing you can send, and a session file or a
short recording of the sound can help too.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<chapter id="commands">
<title>Menus and shortcuts</title>

<para>The menus are behind the <guibutton>☰</guibutton> button in the top
row, or in the menu bar that <keycombo
action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo> shows.</para>

<sect1 id="menu-session">
<title>The Session menu</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>N</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>New Session</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Makes a new, empty session in a tab of its own.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>O</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Session…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens a saved session that is not open.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>All Sessions…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Lists every saved session; see <xref
linkend="all-sessions"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>W</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Close Session</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Closes the session's tab. The session stays saved.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut>&Space;</shortcut><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Run</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Runs the session, or stops it while it runs.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Automatic Connections</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Wires new blocks up as they are added; see <xref
linkend="wiring"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F2</keycap></shortcut><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Rename Session…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Gives the session another name.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Delete Session…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Deletes the session, after asking.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Session</guimenu><guimenuitem>Export Session…</guimenuitem></menuchoice> and <guimenuitem>Import Session…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Write the session to a file, to keep or to give to someone,
and bring a session from such a file in, in a tab of its own.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="menu-edit">
<title>The Edit menu</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Z</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Undo</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Takes back the last change, and says which one it is.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>Z</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Redo</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Makes the change again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Edit</guimenu><guimenuitem>Find Block…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Puts the cursor in the library's search.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="menu-view">
<title>The View menu</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>+</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Zoom In</guimenuitem></menuchoice> and <guimenuitem>Zoom Out</guimenuitem> (<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>-</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>Zoom the canvas.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>0</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Actual Size</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the canvas at 100 %.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>9</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Fit to Window</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Zooms out until the whole session is on screen.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show as List</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the session's blocks as a list instead of on the
canvas.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>View</guimenu><guimenuitem>Blocks</guimenuitem></menuchoice>,
<guimenuitem>Files</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Info</guimenuitem> and
<guimenuitem>Schedule</guimenuitem>
(<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>1</keycap></keycombo>,
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>2</keycap></keycombo>,
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>3</keycap></keycombo>,
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>4</keycap></keycombo>)</term>
<listitem><para>Show that tab of the sidebar.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="menu-settings">
<title>The Settings menu</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Appearance</guisubmenu></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Reverblabr Dark</guimenuitem>,
<guimenuitem>Reverblabr Light</guimenuitem> or <guimenuitem>Follow
System</guimenuitem>; see <xref linkend="window"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>M</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show Menubar</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Shows the menus as a menu bar, or hides it again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Auto Run</guisubmenu></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para><guimenuitem>Once per Login: Stopped Sessions Stay
Stopped</guimenuitem> or <guimenuitem>Every Time the Background Engine
Starts</guimenuitem>: when the sessions set to Auto Run are started; see
<xref linkend="auto-run"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Virtual Devices…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the Virtual Devices window; see <xref
linkend="virtual-devices"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="menu-help">
<title>The Help menu</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F1</keycap></shortcut><guimenu>Help</guimenu><guimenuitem>Handbook</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens this handbook.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Help</guimenu><guimenuitem>Report Bug…</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Opens the place to report a bug; see <xref
linkend="reporting-bugs"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>Help</guimenu><guimenuitem>About Reverblabr</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>The version, the authors and the licences.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

